<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dogū</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as dogu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dogū, &quot;earthen figures&quot;, are small clay figurines of people and animals made in the later part of the Jōmon period of prehistoric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and no longer made in the Yayoi period that followed. Typically 10 to 30 centimetres high, most appear to be female, with big eyes, small waists and wide hips, and many take them to represent goddesses. Some 15,000 to 18,000 are known, from all of Japan except Okinawa and mostly from the east. Their purpose is unknown; some scholars suggest that illnesses were thought to pass into them before they were broken. They are not to be confused with the later haniwa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dogu.jpg" alt="Dogū" width="330" height="535" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dogu_Miyagi_1000_BCE_400_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Dogū, literally earthen figures, are small humanoid and animal figurines made in the later part of the Jōmon period of prehistoric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, which is dated 14,000 to 400 BCE. They come exclusively from the Jōmon period and were no longer made in the Yayoi period that followed. The National Museum of Japanese History estimates that about 15,000 are known, while The Japan Times puts the figure at about 18,000. They were made across all of Japan except Okinawa, and most have been found in eastern Japan; one is rarely found in the west. There are various styles, depending on the area where they were unearthed and on the period. They should not be confused with the clay haniwa funerary objects of the Kofun period.</p><p>The figures are made of clay and are typically 10 to 30 cm high. Most appear to be modelled as female, with big eyes, small waists and wide hips, and many scholars take them to represent goddesses. Many have large abdomens associated with pregnancy, which suggests to some that the Jōmon people saw them as mother goddesses. The Metropolitan Museum of Art says they suggest an association with fertility and shamanistic rites. They tend to have large faces, small arms and hands and compact bodies. Some seem to wear goggles or have heart-shaped faces, and most have marks on the face, chest and shoulders that suggest tattooing and incision with bamboo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The purpose of dogū remains unknown. Some scholars hold that they acted as effigies of people, working by a kind of sympathetic magic. Illnesses, or other misfortune, may have been thought to pass into the figure, which was then destroyed to clear them. Unbroken figures are rare, and most lack an arm, a leg or another part, and in many cases the parts were cut off.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The best known type is the shakōkidogū, or goggle-eyed dogū, so familiar that most Japanese who hear the word dogū picture it. Shakōki literally means light-blocking device, and the name comes from the resemblance of the eyes to traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> snow goggles. These figures have exaggerated buttocks, chests and thighs, and the abdomen is covered with patterns, many of which seem to have been painted with vermilion. The larger ones are hollow. Other types are the heart-shaped or crescent-eyebrow figure, the horned-owl type and the pregnant-woman type.</p><p>Goggle-eyed figures have been found at the Kamegaoka Site in Tsugaru, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aomori-prefecture">Aomori Prefecture</a>, the Teshiromori Site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morioka">Morioka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iwate-prefecture">Iwate Prefecture</a>, the Ebisuda Site in Tajiri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyagi-prefecture">Miyagi Prefecture</a>, and the Izumisawa Kaizuka Site in Ishinomaki, Miyagi Prefecture. All of these sites have been designated Important Cultural Properties.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dogū” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Middle Jōmon also left highly ornate clay figurines, the <b>dogū</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jomon-culture">Jōmon culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
